# Half-B readiness — measuring what was only ever signed

Extends rules 153, 154, 156, 157, 158, 159 and **160**, and replaces none of
them. Rule 157 owns the governance phase. Rule 159 owns the three estate
capacity levels. Rule 160 owns the activation window and the live Half-A
posture. This one owns the **five global prerequisites** and the rule that
governs all of them.

> ## STATUS — READINESS ONLY, HALF B INACTIVE
>
> ```
> single_active_session = TRUE     (Half A, live, all 15 doctors)
> branch_lock           = FALSE    (owner decision; 15 home locks retained, inert)
> HALF_B_ACTIVE         = NO       (pilot cohort; global_permitted source-false)
> HALF_B_APPLY_AUTHORIZED = NO
> governance_phase      = phase_4a
> attestations signed   = 0 of 5
> ```

---

## HB-R1 — A signature never overrides a measurement

`attested true` beside a measurement that is not `PASS` is a **contradiction**
and fails. This is expressed in exactly one place —
`DoctorGlobalEnforcementPrerequisite::contradicts()` — and every consumer asks
there rather than re-deriving it.

## HB-R2 — UNVERIFIED contradicts a signature exactly as FAIL does

"Nobody measured it" is not evidence that it holds. A prerequisite that cannot
be measured is precisely the one a signature must not be allowed to settle.

## HB-R3 — The ABSENCE of a signature fails nothing

A gate that demanded signatures would be red for the whole of a rollout, and a
gate that is red for months gets deleted rather than fixed. Only a
*contradicting* signature fails.

## HB-R4 — `globalPrerequisiteCheck()` reads signatures and nothing else

That is its documented contract and it is not a defect **in that method**. The
defect was that nothing else read a measurement, so
`global_prerequisites_attested` could be satisfied by typing `true` five times.
Do not "fix" it by making it measure — it is NOT_APPLICABLE inside `phase_4a`
and a false signature lands months earlier. The contradiction check is a
**separate, always-evaluated** row.

## HB-R5 — The contradiction check costs nothing until somebody signs

With nothing attested it answers from config alone and issues **zero database
queries** (pinned by test). This scanner runs in CI and in the release-evidence
chain; a governance gate that acquires a database dependency acquires the
ability to redden for reasons that have nothing to do with governance.

## HB-R6 — Once a signature exists, an unreadable measurement is a FAIL

Fail closed. A signature that cannot be checked is exactly the signature that
must not be trusted.

## HB-R7 — Two verdicts, never folded into one

`verdict` (HALF_B_READINESS) is the readiness of the **instruments**.
`activation_prerequisites` is the readiness of the **world**. A green machinery
verdict beside a red world verdict is the expected and correct state: the
instruments are honest and they are saying do not activate. Collapsing them
either reddens the machinery over a purchase order or lets a green headline
imply the fleet is ready.

## HB-R8 — Readiness GO is not APPLY approval

`HALF_B_READINESS_GO=YES` means an activation window may be **planned**. It does
not mean `HALF_B_ACTIVE`, `GLOBAL_FLEET_ENFORCEMENT_ACTIVE` or
`HALF_B_APPLY_AUTHORIZED`. Those need the owner, separately, in their own words.

## HB-R9 — Half B genuinely requires Level 3, and that is hardware not code

`spare_device_available_per_branch` is literally element three of
`android_release.enforcement.global_prerequisites`, so outside `phase_4a` it
must be attested `true` for `global_prerequisites_attested` to pass — and by
HB-R1 it may only be signed once it MEASURES `PASS`. It measures FAIL today.
No sprint closes this by writing code. Do not remove the requirement, and do
not sign it.

## HB-R10 — The rollback restores the CAPTURED posture, never a remembered one

The cohort is the union of two host variables, has no database representation,
and moved inside a single day once already. `DoctorHalfBRollbackProofService`
captures the live values and restores those. A rollback that restores a
constant somebody wrote down restores whatever was true when they wrote it.

## HB-R11 — The rollback subject is a doctor OUTSIDE the cohort

A doctor already inside the pilot cohort is denied before AND after, so they
demonstrate nothing about rolling a **widening** back. If no such doctor exists,
the measurement is UNVERIFIED — never PASS.

## HB-R12 — The rehearsal restores in a `finally`, and that is load-bearing

On the happy path the posture is restored mid-rehearsal, so a test that only
walks the happy path stays green with the `finally` deleted. This was found by
mutation, not by reading. The throw path is where a missing restore leaves a
production process running with global enforcement simulated in its own config.

## HB-R13 — The rehearsal writes nothing and arms nothing

No file, no row, no audit entry, no `config:cache`, no environment value. Every
override lives in the process's own config repository for the length of one
method. It is safe on production; its only database access is reading the doctor
accounts it needs a subject from.

## HB-R14 — An empty population is UNVERIFIED, never PASS

`worst([])` returns UNVERIFIED and `worst()` fails closed on any status outside
its allow-list. "Zero rows, zero problems" is arithmetically a pass and
operationally a broken query, and this programme has already shipped one gate
that reported PASS while the estate held zero usable tablets.

## HB-R15 — Iterate the DECLARED list, never the measurement map

A prerequisite somebody adds to config and nobody implements must surface as
`declared_prerequisite_has_no_producer`, not vanish. That is exactly how this
family of gates went unmeasured for five phases.

## HB-R16 — Production runs cached config

An environment edit alone realizes nothing. Both arming and rollback need a
config-cache rebuild, as `daengtisiams`, in `/var/www/asia-dental-lab-v2`:
`optimize:clear ; config:cache ; route:cache ; view:cache`. Verify with
`foundation:feature-flags --json` and `android:phase4a-pilot-scope --json`.

## HB-R17 — `device_loss_runbook_rehearsed` is evidenced, not queried

No query establishes that a clinic rehearsed losing a tablet. Absent evidence is
UNVERIFIED (the ordinary state of a rung nobody has reached), malformed or
failed evidence is FAIL, an unfilled template is UNVERIFIED, and a rehearsal
dated in the future is FAIL — the direction is checked explicitly because
`diffInDays()` is absolute in some Carbon versions and signed in others.

## HB-R18 — Do not confuse the three estate levels with the five prerequisites

Level 1 (`trusted_device_activation_test_coverage`) gates activation TESTING and
is PASS + signed. Level 2 is reported and gates nothing. Level 3 is
`spare_device_available_per_branch`, which IS one of the five. Rule 159 owns the
levels; this rule owns the list.

## HB-R19 — A cross-check must name a key some engine actually publishes

The first snapshot cross-check compared `authorization_matrix.doctor_count` and
`estate_totals.eligible_doctor_count`. **Neither exists.** It hit its `-1`
sentinel on every real build, returned `agrees => true`, and made its own
demotion unreachable and its finding un-emittable — a documented mechanism that
never ran. Before shipping a comparison, print both sides once and confirm they
are not both the fallback.

## HB-R20 — A missing invariant is UNVERIFIED, never agreement

The same defect failed **open**: absence of evidence was reported as evidence of
agreement. Every "we could not compare" branch in this family resolves to
UNVERIFIED.

## HB-R21 — Derive the checked set from the UNION, never from one list

Both engines filtered the DECLARED list to find signatures, so deleting a name
from `global_prerequisites` while leaving its `true` beside it removed it from
the detector built to catch exactly that. Iterate declared ∪ attested; an
attested name nobody declares is a finding. A signature that is not a boolean
(`1`, `'true'`) is also a finding — code reads "unsigned", a human reads
"signed", and neither reading is safe.

## HB-R22 — Measure the baseline before you move anything

`denyBrowserSessionReason()` returns `null` on its first line when enforcement
is off, so "admitted after rollback" collapsed to *did the boolean we just wrote
read back as false*. Without a starting state, a subject refused for an
unrelated reason is indistinguishable from a broken rollback. Measure the
subject first, and assert the **exact** deny code afterwards — `!== null` is
satisfied by any refusal at all.

## HB-R23 — Never report a property you did not measure, and do not fix it with a projection

`'mutations' => 0` was a hardcoded literal asserting the one property a reader
most wants evidence for. Deriving it from the restoration step looked like the
fix; mutation testing showed it still could not fail independently of that step.
It was **removed**. A redundant projection of an already-reported fact adds a
surface that can drift and no way to catch the drift.

## HB-R24 — State the half a measurement does not cover

The rehearsal reaches the gate through the config repository — resolution step 1
in `FeatureFlagService::resolveOverride()`, strictly above the runtime `env()`
read — so it does **not** exercise the environment file or the config cache. A
deployment whose env-based rollback was inert would still pass it. That limit
lives in the payload, not only in a docblock, and the other half is the
runbook's (HB-R16).

## HB-R25 — The rehearsal's safety rests on three checked facts, not on intuition

Moving `config()` on a live system is safe here only because there is no Octane,
because neither the readiness service nor the scanner that consults it is
reachable from any route or controller, and because an Artisan process serves no
concurrent request. **If any of those changes, re-review before running it on
production** — the second is one controller away from being false.

## HB-R26 — A source-text guard cannot see a transitive mutation

`Phase4aPilotPreparationScanner`'s "inert by construction" test greps the
scanner's own source for forbidden calls. Once a signature exists, the scan
reaches a service that moves enforcement config one `app()` hop away, which that
guard structurally cannot see. The posture-unchanged-after-`scan()` assertion is
what actually holds the guarantee.

